  came before him and as the years  trickled on by and i eventually stumbled  across that interview for homecoming i  realized that tom holland the actor's  journey mirrors peter parker's in many  respects think about it a relatively  unknown kid gets scouted by the biggest  name in town he gets taken under the  wing of their main guy and wants to do  everything he can to not let him down by  doing the best job he can eventually he  might feel as though the mantle is being  passed down to him and now for the first  time this young man needs to figure out  how to man this ship all by himself  without the help of his mentor's guiding  influence all the while his large shadow  looms overhead as the reminder of what  he has to live up to and this is all to  say that tom has as of writing this  video beat in six mainline mcu  blockbusters and had his own trilogy of  films by the age of 25 still by the way  younger than toby and andrew both were  when they first put on the suit just  something i thought was interesting to  keep in mind but for now on to the  review of far from home  does anyone have any neighborhood  questions what is it like to take over  from tony stark there's some big shoes  to fill  this scene right here summarizes the  conflict in this film in a nutshell if  homecoming was all about peter exploring  and accepting the strengths he has as  peter parker in other words trying to  eliminate any illusion he might have  about himself then far from home is a  story detailing how peter has to learn  to see his mentor for who he truly was  not as a deity without fault but as a  gifted man that struggled and failed  just as much or more than any other  person in peter's life in other words he  must learn to not hold himself to some  impossibly high standard and to stop  doubting himself when he inevitably  fails in living up to that impossibly  high standard to stop looking at tony  through rose tinted glasses but to quite  literally look through the eyes of the  man tony believes he could be that's  what far from home is ultimately about  at the end of the day and once again how  disney and marvel have elected to  leverage tony for the purposes of this  film is sensational and a perfect  epilogue to the infinity saga which  concludes in this film in terms of  pacing this is up there with the  absolute best  the selling point of this movie is  spider-man in europe and boy does it  deliver on that front in keeping with  the mcu's dedication to help this  spider-man feel distinct from the older  incarnations they are keeping him as far  away as possible from the skyscrapers of  new york city and instead have him  occupying hostels in venice navigating  festivals in prague and drone striking  on alpine motorways and that's just the  first third of the film i think my  favorite part of this movie at least in  the first half before the story finally  shows its hand is how much john hughes's  influence can be felt within john  hughes's films there's always a  concerted effort to bring to light the  individual humanity in as many  characters as possible whether they be  high school kids or a middle-aged  businessman from chicago trying to get  home in time for the holidays and in far  from home you can really feel that  driving the dialogue and indeed the  narrative in some places ned and betty's  relationship is hilarious and cute  brad's motivation to stop peter from  connecting with mj on this trip is  filled with hijinks and mysterio's  ultimate motivation once it's revealed  is understandable it's not right but we  can empathize with the position he was  put in by tony stark which further  speaks to his flaws as a human being  that peter needs to learn to accept  quentin beck is in this film a villain  specifically because of tony he's proof  that tony isn't perfect and speaking of  mysterio can we just take a second to  appreciate how brilliant jake i had to  google the spelling of his name  gyllenhaal was in this film there's a  bit of a mystery surrounding mysterio  you don't say towards the beginning of  this film however due to my being a fan  of the old spider-man games and 90s  cartoon i knew full well who mysterio  was which did take the fun out of what  ultimately was a great reveal in the bar  with mysterio to get peter's edith  glasses from him however i did get to  experience the genuine surprise on my  wife's face when i showed her that scene  so there's that prior to the reveal  quentin beck or mysterio played on  peter's biggest insecurity his belief  that he can live up to tony's legacy and  in playing up to that he becomes exactly  what peter thinks he needs a mentor  figure to hand over all of this  responsibility too and not for no reason  but because for the first time since  becoming a superhero he's happy to just  be peter parker he has other priorities  you look really pretty  therefore i have value no  no that's not i'm messing with you  you look pretty too  nowadays there seems to be a ton of love  out there for zendaya as an act and in  light of this i find myself in the  position to say i too love zendaya now  brave as a statement that is i do have  an explanation for my take and  furthermore i wasn't always in favor of  her version of mj in this series for the  longest time i was lukewarm on it and  this change of mind came about courtesy  of a specific creator here on youtube  that i highly encourage anyone who's  interested in learning more about  spider-man to check out and his name is  implicitly pretentious in one of his  many videos deconstructing the topic he  drew specific attention to something i  found deeply interesting and eye-opening  whenever i watch a film or write a  review i'm always looking for another  way to examine something that might  offer with it a renewed perspective or a  deeper understanding of the approach a  filmmaker might take when making a movie  and in one of his videos he discusses  the broad topic of comic to film  adaptation with focus on the character  mary jane watson specifically through a  modern lens in my review for mark webbs  the amazing spider-man i drew specific  attention to webb's incarnation or  approach to adapting the character of  peter parker for a modern era as  mentioned in that video toby moore  closely resembles what peter parker  would wear aesthetically in the 60s but  the attitude and get up andrew carried  through his performance ran closer to  authentic for what peter parker might be  if he was written for the year 2012.  this is in effect why i love zendaya's  performance as michelle jones or mj you  might have noticed that in my review for  sam remy's trilogy i praise kirsten  dunst's performance and her dynamic with  toby however i did not praise it for its  comic accuracy like i did garfield's  performance in the comics mary jane is  quite progressive for the 1960s when she  was introduced to the world and while  everything from the hair to the clothes  kirsten wore in the rainy films felt  true to the character in a classical  sense it failed to capture the spirit of  the character which was her progressive  and exciting spirit in fact that  incarnation of the character felt sort  of i don't know conventional or sort of  safe when i frame it that way and so  enter mj from the mcu understanding what  i do now concerning mary jane from the  original comics mj in the mcu is exactly  what i would envision a modern take  channeling the spirit of that character  might sound and act like this is all to  say i love her performance in this and  while i don't feel as connected or  invested in her relationship with peter  as i did with gwen stacey and emma stone  i can absolutely respect what she brings  to this variation of the character and  i'm majorly impressed  quentin beck or mysterio is not only a  visually exciting choice to attack peter  parker within this film with but is also  i think by design a brilliant metaphor  to represent the issues peter parker has  to overcome in this story i mentioned at  the beginning of this section that  homecoming was the story of peter coming  to terms with his strength and worth as  an individual peter parker independent  of all the bells and whistles tony's  mentorship afforded him that he has  value that he is a hero in that same  breath far from home explores the  natural continuation or evolution on  that idea in peter now having found a  new lease on life as a civilian who  wants to try and embrace and enjoy a  normal life for a change he's confronted  time and again with either massive  depictions of iron man at school or in  the real world and when he dons the mask  as spider-man people clamber to ask him  if he's the next in line to lead the  avengers and to him he thinks this is  ridiculous he doesn't believe he can  because to him he cannot possibly live  up to what iron man represented to him  and so in this film he struggles to  overcome that image or illusion of tony  stark in his mind in order to see the  reality of the situation and who better  to face peter parker and his grappling  with reality than the master of  illusions mysterio and the visuals  surrounding these illusions are utterly  spectacular with a capital s watching  these illusion sequences in the theater  left my jaw firmly planted on the floor  particularly when iron man came crawling  out of the ground as a zombie as an  illusion literally haunting peter  eventually and thankfully this leads to  a beautiful coming together with happy  where he confesses to peter that tony  like peter always second guessed himself  but the suaven cam front was exactly  that a front and upon receiving this  news and getting a new lease on life the  look happy gives to peter as he's  working away on the plan for the final  battle warms my heart and brings a tear  to my eye every single time and speaking  of that final battle and confrontation  between peter and mysterio it's still up  there as one of my favorite spider-man  moments out of all the films i've spoken  about across all three videos i've made  on spider-man the choreography is  incredible the visuals are appropriately  massive and it has a swerve at the end i  honestly didn't see coming but was  totally perfect for someone like beck to  try and pull off however my favorite  aspect of this big finish was the  philosophy behind its design this is a  movie about seeing people for who they  truly are whether they be villains  allies mentors or indeed yourself to not  allow yourself to be consumed by those  illusions and so the dependence on  peter's spider sense during this film  was incredibly refreshing and frankly  inspired with it at the beginning of the  film acting up according to ant-may  representing his inability to see  through the illusion of tony stark's  shadow in other words the incorporation  of the spider sense i think acts as a  beautiful metaphor illustrating that in  order to take control of this situation  and to save the day peter can't rely on  a catch net iron man can't save him  another hero isn't going to come out of  the woodwork he must be willing to look  deep within himself to gain clarity to  trust his intuition to trust his senses  to trust himself and to accept that tony  chose him for a reason it's brilliant  it's beautiful it's poetic it's so damn  spider-man and it's a perfect segue into  spider-man no way home  spider-man no way home spider-man no way  home is special and as you can see for  the purposes of this review i've elected  to change the format up a little bit for  this section and i highly encourage  anyone who hasn't yet seen no way home  for themselves in theaters to do so if  they have the means because i sincerely  believe that this is the best  live-action spider-man film made in over  15 years and since spider-man 2 back in  2004 no way home is the first spider  flick i've seen in theaters more than  once in fact i went to go see it three  times i would have gone a fourth time  but arlen went a little crazy with  kovitt over december and well my ticket  got refunded this is all to say that i  believe spider-man no way home is a  terrific film that made me tear up made  me laugh and kept me at the edge of my  seat from start to finish and contained  within it are elements that make not  just for a terrific film but the perfect  ending to this trilogy of videos i've  written over the last month in  celebration of the live-action  spider-man i've grown up with so what  are we waiting for let's dive right in  as this film continues from the  conclusion of far from home without  missing a beat you could put these two  films back to back and they'd flow  perfectly furthermore the pacing in this  film was a little lopsided with the  first half feeling distinctly slower  than the second i will say that the  first half isn't slowed by any  conventional means either the first 10  minutes deals with a great deal  including the fallout of peter's  identity being revealed to matt freaking  murdock's introduction to the mcu films  to happy and may's relationship troubles  to what this film's main driving force  will be peter's desire to help his  friends better their circumstances at  any cost unlike any of the other films  in the mcu home trilogy this film also  feels distinct due to its lack of focus  or involvement of iron man in any  capacity the first had him directly  involved the second it was indirectly  and now this his presence can't be felt  outside of some extraneous tech here and  there which further adds to this feeling  of isolation and dwindling options for  peter to utilize in order to better his  friends chances when applying for  college and that's one thing this film  does a great job with right off the bat  this concept may or may not have worked  in the comics however when adapted  correctly into the modern day the court  of public opinion as murdoch puts it is  in full effect people swarm mj and peter  initially a woman shouts out that peter  hit her and as they jump to safety a man  is seen leaping after them to compound  this protests are held outside of  peter's school bricks are thrown through  his window and literally everyone in his  life starts to treat him either as a god  or a murderous criminal and this is the  strongest aspect of the film's first  half it really made me feel for peter  and ultimately understand where his  thought process was when he was reaching  out to stephen strange  this is where the story really starts to  take shape and again with a time check  we're only 20 minutes into the feature  i've seen a lot of critics claim that  the pacing in this first half of the  film is slow but in reality i think it's  quite fast and satisfying and my only  explanation for people feeling  differently is that they compare it to  what comes in the second half or their  anticipation of what comes in the second  half should i say and i think that's  largely it because let's face it it was  the worst kept secret in hollywood in  late 2021 one strangest spell flops and  accidentally releases a ton of villains  from past spider-man stories the fun  really starts to ramp up with references  and interesting interactions as the  order of the day dr octopus vs peter on  the bridge was choreographed  spectacularly well with some really  interesting ideas at play concerning his  iron spider suit fittingly the method  peter ends up using to overcome octavius  in this instance is a difference that he  uniquely possesses when compared to any  other spider-man also with his suit  moving to protect his chest it  additionally to looking awesome and  being an interesting way to reveal  peter's identity to otto it also plays  into the theme of peter consistently  shedding more and more of tony's  materials working towards that  independent outcome that the film  concludes with on the topic of villains  however let's call a spade a spade  because not all villains are created  equal in this flick flint marco and dr  connors are both in this film acting at  the sandman and lizard respectively  however their involvement particularly  the lizards is quite removed when we  compare it to more involved characters  like electro or dr octopus and speaking  of electro in this film i found him so  much more entertaining than he was in  the amazing spider-man 2. now that's not  to say that this version of him would  have worked better for that movie i  honestly would have a difficult time  trying to convince myself of that  however his suave and much more witty  persona is perfectly fitting for so many  interactions we get to see in this  feature particularly in happy's  apartment where we get a chance to  explore so many interactions we thought  we'd never see  and on the point of villains i had to  say something outright and without an  ounce of hyperbola i'm not sure if  anyone else out there will agree with me  on this but william defoe's performance  of green goblin and norman osborne in no  way home might be better than his  performance in the first spider-man  flick with toby and let me be completely  clear here i loved both but i thought he  had so much more to work with in this  film and was afforded the chance to go  even further with his insanity too like  that seat in the apartment where peter's  senses are triggered and he dukes it out  with the goblin that scene is  oh chef's kiss brilliant i mean when he  punches him time and again and the  goblin just laughs in his face man  that's a moment that still gives me  chills and not even to make mention of  the ending or  okay  breath let's talk about what norman  offers this story because this movie  simply wouldn't land or work as well  without him out of all the villains used  in this film none is more important or  contributes more to peter's journey than  norman one of the best choices in this  film comes by way of peter's motivation  to save all of these villains it's such  a perfectly character-driven choice that  speaks to his aunt may's philosophy  which  comes to a screaming and haunting  conclusion in this film's turning point  yielding the single most important quote  to live by in peter's life that with  great power  must also come great responsibility and  this took me off guard both  intellectually and emotionally going off  of peter's very first conversation with  tony in his bedroom back in civil war i  always assumed in the six months leading  up to that conversation his uncle ben  scenario had already yielded that  outcome for him as he loosely speaks of  that same responsibility in that film to  tony it's what convinces him to recruit  the kid in the first place but similar  to the garfield film it never sat right  with me that that quote wasn't there but  thankfully now we can say that it is and  when that moment hit like the train that  it was it made me realize perhaps the  most important aspect of this story this  isn't just the culmination of a trilogy  of spider-man films but the culmination  of peter parker's origin in the mcu  with his final trial coming in the shape  of villains from the past and present  offering him a moral dilemma one that he  needs to answer with mercy which after  what norman did to his aunt may seems  like an almost impossibility which  brings me to everyone's favorite part of  this film the bringing together of toby  andrew and tom's respective spider-man  to one location and to share a heart  to heart one of my biggest worries going  into this film was over how they might  mishandle or fail to deliver upon what  was a fascinating angle to tackle the  darkest moment in this young character's  life but this delivers in more ways than  i could have hoped to describe and to  that point this is one of the darkest  marvel movies point blank dealing with  themes of death anger revenge  responsibility and isolation with this  scene where toby and andrew support tom  being perhaps the best scene in the film  and let me tell you andrew freaking  garfield almost steals the damn movie  tom holland and william defoe share what  are perhaps career defining performances  however after seeing this film three  times in theaters one of the most  standout aspects of the movie still  remains andrew's tortured portrayal of a  defeated peter parker following  the events of the amazing spider-man 2  struggling and desperately clawing to  what semblance of hope he has left in  him trying to hold on to gwen's wish for  him to never lose hope he doesn't have a  ton of lines in this film but this  spectacular actor maximizes his minutes  in this movie let me tell you leaving me  honestly in a situation where i feel  like begging sony for the amazing  spider-man 3 to give this poor character  some closure and speaking of other  spider-man watching toby bounce back and  forth with andrew was special and to  tell you the truth made me feel like a  teenager again and similar to the  villains what this film does extremely  well is allow time for these characters  to hang out and just shoot the breeze  and to tell you the truth i'm counting  down the seconds to when this becomes  available on disney plus or blu-ray  because i want to watch it again which  brings me to the ending and this was and  let me tell you in all honesty  perhaps my favorite ending to any  spider-man film ever  now that might be subject to change over  the course of the next year as this film  settles deep into my subconscious but  there's just so much to love about this  part of the movie getting to see toby  suit up again andrew redeeming himself  by saving mj and electro wishing  spider-man was black all really cool  nods to the past and future of this  franchise but perhaps more than anything  else what i love about this section is  the final battle between goblin and  spider-man with subtle touches like  peter going for a punch and us as an  audience really feeling that impact  telling us that not only is peter  serious in this instance but unlike  earlier in the film where these pulled  punches made goblin's face light up with  laughter now they make him laugh for a  whole other reason they're an indication  that goblin is getting to him and that  peter is playing right into his hands  but toby  stops him  the anger in tom's face crumbles to  sorrow it's a brilliant scene that  climaxes with peter making the right  decision in the end  saving norman  over the years one of the main critiques  of this spider-man that i've noticed has  been his dependence on the abilities and  accessories of other people  predominantly iron man so much so that  it's led to numerous people over the  internet labelled tom's portrayal as  iron boy or some variation thereof and  while i understand that sentiment i  can't help but praise marvel's  dedication to this idea it offered tom a  tremendous way into the mcu with one of  the most popular characters it offered  him a unique trauma that he alone had to  deal with and perhaps my favorite aspect  of his alignment with iron man is the  visual progression he demonstrates  throughout the films as he becomes less  and less dependent on the stark tech  until finally he reaches the natural end  point for spider-man a down on his luck  teen living in new york city alone  wearing a costume he made living with  all the decisions and mistakes he's made  in solitude it's a haunting but perfect  way to end this trilogy with a kid that  was once desperate for recognition in  homecoming after going through  tremendous loss and growth developed  into the adult we see today totally  willing to forgo any and all recognition  quite literally for the sake and  well-being of his family his friends and  the world and looking back through this  trilogy and his involvement in the mcu i  can't say i would change anything it  truly was in every sense of the word a  celebration and a love letter to the  spectacular and the amazing spider-man  growing up spider-man helped me to  become a better person and the only way  peter parker spider-man and indeed  marvel can consistently achieve that  valuable effect it has on people is by  allowing their characters to grow change  and adapt to the social ecosystems of  the era they find themselves in and in  that respect i think tom holland's  version of that character is as  important iconic and as valuable to the  mainstream idea of spider-man as toby  and andrew's contributions however if  marvel and disney maintain their  trajectory with this character in tom  holland spider-man it is my belief that  he will be someday the definitive  spider-man not just for the new  generation but perhaps the old one too  furthermore this won't be my last  spider-man video alright i had plans to  review into the spiderverse as part of  this by december but i think i'm going  to wait for its sequel to release so i  can give my thoughts on both of them  together because honestly that first  film on its own deserves to be part of a  big video like these ones did so if you  want to see that video or more videos  like this be sure to like and subscribe  it doesn't cost you anything and it  really makes my day with that said i  hope you enjoyed this spidey sember and  i thank you so much for watching  [Music]  watch as we all fly away  [Music]

